  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nFairly vigorous shortwave seen on water vapor loop rounding the\nback side of the upper trough and moving into ND. Regional radar\nshows some showers with the shortwave, with most of the\nsignificant returns up in Canada. NDAWN network has yet to show\nand precipitation in ND reaching the ground, and that fits the\nQPF forecasts for the most of the high resolution models. Very\nlittle instability across the area, so if any precipitation does\nreach the ground it will be little more than some light\nsprinkles. A stronger shortwave approaches tonight and moves\ninto Manitoba and Ontario tomorrow, helping to push a cold front\nThursday night into the forecast area. Many of the models try \nto develop some showers and thunderstorms out ahead of that\nboundary Thursday afternoon and evening, but CAPE values are\nexpected to be in the 500-1000 J/kg and low level moisture not\nthat great. Currently the HREF has some 20 to 30 percent\nprobability for thunder, but updraft helicities indicating\nsevere are pretty minimal. There does look to be some low level\nand elevated smoke moving into the area ahead of the front,\nso increased high cloud cover a bit along with some patchy\nsmoke. \n\nFriday looks fairly quiet with high pressure building in behind\nthe front. Northwesterly flow aloft sets up for the weekend into\nthe first part of the work week. Various weak embedded\nshortwaves could bring some precipitation chances, but all\nsolutions look pretty light and timing and strength of such\nminor features is hard to tell at this point anyway. Temps look\nnear seasonal values.",
